        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "003c6f7f-cff1-50ce-920c-17fadbbaa78a",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Companies: Black Star Line\n- People: Marcus Garvey, Leo H. Healy\n- Organizations: Universal Negro Improvement Association (UNIA)\n- Concepts: Back-to-Africa movement, Colonization movement, Black Zionism\n\nFacts:\n- The Back-to-Africa movement, also known as the Colonization movement or Black Zionism, originated in the United States in the 19th century.\n- The Black Star Line operated from 1919 to 1922 as a shipping line incorporated by Marcus Garvey and other members of the Universal Negro Improvement Association.\n- The Black Star Line became a key part of Marcus Garvey's contribution to the Back-to-Africa movement.\n- In 1923, Leo H. Healy, an attorney for the Black Star Line, was a key government witness in the trial of Marcus Garvey.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "b3d6b6f8-dc21-50bd-b381-24ffee693e04",
        "text": "Page: Black Star Line. Sentence: The Black Star Line (1919−1922) was a shipping line incorporated by Marcus Garvey, the organizer of the Universal Negro Improvement Association (UNIA), and other members of the UNIA.\nPage: Black Star Line. Sentence: Black Star Line became a key part of Garvey's contribution to the Back-to-Africa movement.\nPage: Back-to-Africa movement. Sentence: The Back-to-Africa movement, also known as the Colonization movement or Black Zionism, originated in the United States in the 19th century.\nPage: Leo H. Healy. Sentence: He was an attorney for the Black Star Line and in 1923 he was a key government witness in the trial of Marcus Garvey.",
